<!-- PREAMBLE: THE RULING -->
<div class="preamble-box">
  <strong>The Halachic Ruling (from the <em>Shulchan Aruch</em> — the standard code of Jewish law):</strong> For every transgression in the Torah, one should transgress rather than be killed — except for idolatry, sexual immorality, and murder. And in public — before ten Jews — one is obligated to be killed rather than transgress if the non-Jew intends to make him violate his religion, even for something as minor as the strap of a sandal. And similarly in a time of religious persecution — see the <em>Shulchan Aruch</em>.
  <span class="source-ref">This halacha is grounded in the discourse <em>"Tik'u"</em> (Sound the Shofar) — <em>Likutay Maharan Tinyana</em> (The Collected Torah of our Master, Rabbi Nachman, Second Part), discourse 4, section 3 — as cited above in <em>Hilchos Maachalai Akum</em> (The Laws of Food of a Non-Jew), Halacha 2. See there what our Rebbe, of blessed memory, said.</span>
</div>

